From 13278a64c34d91815b5a397f22f8abc1ba396b40 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artyom Shalkhakov <artyom.shalkhakov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:57:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] libs-base: NSExpression's selector functions, fetch requests
 and key paths

NSExpression had one kind of function expression, the named kind - sum:,
uppercase:, and the rest, each an implementation of its own in
NSPredicate.m. Core Data's mapping models are written in three forms this
library did not have, and a mapping model compiled by Xcode could not be
read, let alone followed.

FUNCTION(receiver, "selector:", arguments...) sends a selector to the
object an expression evaluates to. A mapping model fills a relationship
with one of these, asking the migration manager for the destination
instances of a source object's related objects.

FETCH(request, context, countOnly) runs a fetch request against a context
and answers the rows, or their number. It is how a mapping says which
objects it applies to, the request itself built by a FUNCTION on the
manager, so the two are wanted together or not at all.

A key path reached through something else - $source.author - OS X writes
as a key path expression with that something as its operand. This library
read the path and dropped the operand, so $source.author meant author,
evaluated against whatever object happened to be passed; where that was
not the source object, the value was nil. It is read as the key path
composition it is now, and written the way OS X writes it rather than as
a binary expression of its own, so each framework reads the other's.

All three are parsed, printed, evaluated, archived and read back. The
selector form is sent through an NSInvocation, one argument per colon,
and a result that is not an object comes back as an NSNumber. The fetch
form knows nothing of Core Data - this library cannot - so it sends
-executeFetchRequest:error: or -countForFetchRequest:error: to whatever
the context expression evaluates to, which a test can answer as well as a
managed object context can. Its keys and class name in an archive are the
ones OS X uses; Core Data declares the type constant and the class, so
neither is added here.

An archived function expression carries an operand, which until now was
written as a placeholder standing for the class OS X implements the named
functions on, and ignored when read. It is read now: a placeholder still
means a named function, and a real receiver means the selector form, so
archives from either framework come back as what they were.

The test added here cannot run on an unpatched library: it names the two
new methods, so it fails to compile rather than to pass.
